---

<Doc
  title="Help · Velvet"
  description="Solutions for the usual problems with Velvet, the YouTube client for macOS: the first start, playback, updates and sign-in."
  label="Help"
  heading="Solutions for the usual problems"
  lede="If your problem is not here, report it. The last section tells you how."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="first-open">macOS stops Velvet when you open it the first time</h2>
  <p>
    Apple does not notarize Velvet. Notarization needs a paid developer account. Because of this, macOS stops a downloaded copy
    the first time that you open it.
  </p>
  <p>This does not occur when you install Velvet with Homebrew.</p>
  <p>To open Velvet, do one of these two procedures.</p>
  <h3>Procedure 1: System Settings</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <strong>System Settings</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>.</li>
    <li>Go to <strong>Security</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Procedure 2: Terminal</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Terminal.</li>
    <li>
      Do this command:
      <pre><code>xattr -cr /Applications/Velvet.app</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>Start Velvet.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="which-download">Find the correct download for your Mac</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the Apple menu.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>About This Mac</strong>.</li>
    <li>If you see <strong>Chip</strong>, your Mac has Apple silicon. Use the Apple silicon download.</li>
    <li>If you see <strong>Processor</strong>, your Mac has an Intel processor. Use the Intel download.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The Homebrew installation selects the correct version for your Mac.</p>

  <h2 id="first-start">The player shows a download on the first start</h2>
  <p>
    Velvet needs two tools to play videos: yt-dlp and a JavaScript runtime (Deno). On the first start, Velvet downloads
    these tools. The progress shows at the bottom of the window.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you open a video before the downloads are complete, the player shows the progress. The video starts when the
    downloads are complete.
  </p>

  <h2 id="no-playback">Videos do not play</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube changes frequently. A change at YouTube can stop playback in Velvet. A new version of yt-dlp usually
    corrects the problem.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Push Command-comma to open the settings.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Advanced</strong>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Check Now</strong>.</li>
    <li>Play the video again.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If the video does not play after this procedure, <a href="#report">report the problem</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="buffering">A video stops frequently to load</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube sets a speed limit for each video link. On a slow connection, 4K video and HDR video can stop to load.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the video stops frequently, Velvet decreases the quality by one step. A quality that you select yourself does
    not change.
  </p>
  <p>To prevent the stops, select a lower quality in <strong>Settings › Playback</strong>.</p>

  <h2 id="search-history">The search box shows only a few past searches</h2>
  <p>
    The full search history comes from the Google My Activity page. If Google changes this page, Velvet shows a shorter
    history from YouTube.
  </p>
  <p>Make sure that the search history is on in <strong>Settings › General</strong>.</p>

  <h2 id="pip-timeline">You cannot drag the timeline in Picture in Picture</h2>
  <p>
    The Picture in Picture window of macOS does not let you drag its timeline. Use the buttons that go back 10 seconds
    and forward 10 seconds. Other players have the same limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Velvet also has its own floating window, which has a seek bar. You can select it in the Picture in Picture settings
    in <strong>Settings › Playback</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="intel">Intel Macs</h2>
  <p>
    Intel Macs cannot decode AV1 video in hardware. Most Intel Macs from 2017 or later can decode VP9 video in
    hardware. Velvet selects a codec that your Mac can decode in hardware.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    When version 1.0 was released, nobody had tested the Intel version on an Intel Mac. If you use it, tell us the
    result in an <a href={`${repo}/issues/new`}>issue</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="updates">Update Velvet</h2>
  <p>
    Velvet looks for a new version one time each day. When a new version is available, <strong
      >Settings › About</strong
    > shows the release notes and an <strong>Install and Relaunch</strong> button.
  </p>
  <p>To look for a new version at a different time, select <strong>Velvet › Check for Updates…</strong>.</p>
  <p>Before Velvet installs an update, it does these checks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The download is complete.</li>
    <li>The download has the same signature as the installed app.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a check fails, Velvet does not install the update.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/changelog">changelog</a> shows what changed in each version.</p>

  <h2 id="sign-out">Remove your sign-in</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Push Command-comma to open the settings.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Account</strong>.</li>
    <li>Sign out.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Velvet then removes your session cookies from your Mac. Refer to <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="report">Report a problem</h2>
  <p>Open an <a href={`${repo}/issues/new`}>issue</a> on GitHub. Include this information:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The Velvet version. You can find it in <strong>Settings › About</strong>.</li>
    <li>The macOS version.</li>
    <li>What you did, what you expected, and what occurred.</li>
    <li>For a playback problem, the video link.</li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
